The BMS inputs need to be filtered in different ways for different functions.
Calibration parameter:
N_slo = 10
N_fast = 5

Slow inputs
For the slow state of charge (SOC) function that runs at 200ms, the current, cell voltage and cell temperature need to be filtered accordingly.
A simple low pass filter (FIR) is used for that purpose:
Current_(filt-slo) (k)=  (I(k)+I(k-1)+I(k-2)+⋯+I(k-N-1))/(N_slo)
I(k) is the current measured at high frequency from the shunt.

cellVolt_(filt-slo) (k)=  (cellV(k)+cellV(k-1)+cellV(k-2)+⋯+cellV(k-N-1))/(N_slo)
cellV(k) is the cell voltage measured at high frequency from the AFE board.
The same applied to the temperature measurement.

Fast inputs
For the faster state of power (SOP) function that runs at 100ms, the current, cell voltage and cell temperature need to be filtered accordingly.
A simple low pass filter (FIR) is used for that purpose:
Current_(filt-fast) (k)=  (I(k)+I(k-1)+I(k-2)+⋯+I(k-N-1))/(N_fast)
I(k) is the current measured at high frequency from the shunt.

cellVolt_(filt-fast) (k)=  (cellV(k)+cellV(k-1)+cellV(k-2)+⋯+cellV(k-N-1))/(N_fast)
cellV(k) is the cell voltage measured at high frequency from the AFE board.
The same applied to the temperature measurement.

Whenever a function uses current & voltage, it is very important to consistently pick either slow or fast filtering but never mix them. The reason for this is that current and voltage measurements must be synchronized.
